To reverse the order of an array in JavaScript, the most straightforward and efficient way is to use the built-in `Array.prototype.reverse()` method. This method reverses the elements of an array **in place**, meaning it modifies the original array. If you want to preserve the original array and only get a reversed copy, you can create a copy of the array first.

---

### ✅ Using the Built-in `reverse()` Method

```javascript
const arr = [3, 2, 1];
arr.reverse();
console.log(arr); // Output: [1, 2, 3]
```

**Note:**  
This modifies the original array `arr`. If you'd like to keep the original array unchanged, use a copy before reversing:

```javascript
const arr = [3, 2, 1];
const reversedArr = arr.slice().reverse();
console.log(reversedArr); // Output: [1, 2, 3]
console.log(arr);         // Output: [3, 2, 1] (unchanged)
```

---

### ✅ Manual Reversal Without Built-in Methods

If you prefer not to use the built-in `reverse()` method, you can reverse the array manually by swapping elements from the start and end of the array:

```javascript
function reverseArray(arr) {
  const n = arr.length;
  for (let i = 0; i < n / 2; i++) {
    const temp = arr[i];
    arr[i] = arr[n - 1 - i];
    arr[n - 1 - i] = temp;
  }
  return arr;
}

const arr = [3, 2, 1];
const reversed = reverseArray(arr);
console.log(reversed); // Output: [1, 2, 3]
```

**Note:**  
This also modifies the original array. To avoid that, make a copy before passing it to the function:

```javascript
const arr = [3, 2, 1];
const reversed = reverseArray([...arr]);
console.log(reversed); // [1, 2, 3]
console.log(arr);      // [3, 2, 1] (unchanged)
```

---

### ✅ Using the Spread Operator and `reverse()`

Another concise way to get a reversed copy is using the spread operator:

```javascript
const arr = [3, 2, 1];
const reversed = [...arr].reverse();
console.log(reversed); // [1, 2, 3]
```

---

### ✅ Summary

| Method | Modifies Original Array | Notes |
|--------|--------------------------|-------|
| `reverse()` | ✅ Yes | Simple and efficient |
| `slice().reverse()` | ❌ No | Preserves the original array |
| Manual Swapping | ✅ Yes | Useful for learning purposes |
| Spread + `reverse()` | ❌ No | Clean and concise |

Choose the method that best fits your use case. If you're working with a read-only array or want to avoid side effects, always create a copy before reversing.

